<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dniester</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dniester is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, about 1,360 kilometres long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi</a>. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as the Tyras, it ran through the lands of many ancient peoples and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. It has long served as a frontier, and today divides most of Moldova from Transnistria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dniester.jpg" alt="Dniester" width="298" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: permission is in the lower right corner of the page · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dniester01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moldavia took shape in the mid-fourteenth century in the lands between the Eastern Carpathians and the <b>Dniester</b>, recently freed from the control of the Golden Horde.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its roots lie in the Starčevo–Körös–Criș and Vinča cultures, with influence from the Bug–<b>Dniester</b> culture and, in its early period, from the Linear Pottery culture to the north and the Boian culture to the south.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yamnaya culture, also called the Pit Grave or Ochre Grave culture, is the name archaeologists give to a late Copper Age to early Bronze Age culture of the Pontic–Caspian steppe, between the Southern Bug, <b>Dniester</b> and Ural rivers, dated to about 3300–2600 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Empire recognised Russia's annexation of Crimea and ceded the coast between the Bug and <b>Dniester</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Antes appear in the 6th century in Procopius and Jordanes, who describe them as a people related to the Sclaveni living east of the <b>Dniester</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/antes">Antes</a></li><li><blockquote>Known mainly from Roman sources, the Costoboci were a Dacian people living beyond Roman Dacia, probably in the upper <b>Dniester</b> and Prut region of modern Ukraine and Romania, in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/costoboci">Costoboci</a></li></ul></section>
